<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fifteenth Amendment to the United States Constitution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1870">1870</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Fifteenth Amendment</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Fifteenth Amendment to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">United States Constitution</a>, ratified in 1870, forbids the federal and state governments to deny a citizen the right to vote because of 'race, color, or previous condition of servitude'. The last of the three Reconstruction amendments, it enfranchised Black men across the country. From the 1890s Southern states evaded it with poll taxes, literacy tests and violence, and its promise was largely realised only with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voting-rights-act-of-1965">Voting Rights Act of 1965</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The amendment grew from Congress's debates over the rights of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> after the Civil War. Once the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirteenth-amendment">Thirteenth Amendment</a> ended the three-fifths rule, Republicans feared that the Democratic-dominated Southern states would gain House seats, and hoped to offset this by attracting and protecting black votes. The Civil Rights Act of 1866 was passed over President Johnson's veto, and the Fourteenth Amendment was adopted on 28 July 1868. Its second section only reduced a state's representation if it disenfranchised male citizens, which left states free to deny ballot access by race. In 1868 only eight Northern states allowed black men to vote. The election of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant">Ulysses S. Grant</a> that year convinced a majority of Republicans that protecting black male voters mattered to the party's future.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Section 1 provides that the right of citizens to vote shall not be denied or abridged by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> or any State on account of race, color or previous condition of servitude. Section 2 gives Congress power to enforce the amendment by appropriate legislation.</p><p>Representative John Bingham pushed for a wider ban, but a proposal covering nativity, property, education and religion was rejected, as was one banning literacy tests. A conference committee drew up the narrow final text, which mentions no poll taxes and does not guarantee the right to hold office. The House approved it on 25 February 1869 by 144 to 44 and the Senate the next day by 39 to 13, almost entirely along party lines. Senator Charles Sumner abstained because it did not prohibit literacy tests and poll taxes.</p><p>The amendment divided the women's suffrage movement, since it barred race discrimination but not sex discrimination. The American Equal Rights Association split into the National Woman Suffrage Association of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susan-b-anthony">Susan B. Anthony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-cady-stanton">Elizabeth Cady Stanton</a>, who opposed it, and the American Woman Suffrage Association of Lucy Stone and Henry Browne Blackwell, who supported it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nevada">Nevada</a> ratified first, on 1 March 1869, and Congress required <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> to ratify as a condition of regaining representation. It was certified as part of the Constitution on 30 March 1870.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Supreme Court interpreted the amendment narrowly in the late 19th century. From 1890 to 1910 Southern Democrats adopted new state constitutions and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jim-crow-laws">Jim Crow laws</a>, and poll taxes, literacy tests, white primaries and violent intimidation by the Ku Klux Klan disenfranchised most black voters and many poor whites, while grandfather clauses exempted white men.</p><p>In the 20th century the Court struck down grandfather clauses in Guinn v. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1915 and dismantled the white primary in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> primary cases between 1927 and 1953. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voting-rights-act-of-1965">Voting Rights Act of 1965</a> provided federal oversight of elections in discriminatory jurisdictions and banned literacy tests.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fifteenth Amendment to the United States Constitution” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The abolition of slavery by the Thirteenth Amendment in 1865 was followed by the Fourteenth and <b>Fifteenth Amendments</b>, which promised citizenship, the equal protection of the laws and the vote regardless of race.</blockquote><a href="/movement/civil-rights-movement">Civil rights movement · Background</a></li></ul></section>
